// GENERATED by scripts/build-guides.mjs from docs/guides/*.md and templates/**/*.json.
// Do not edit by hand: edit the source file and run `npm run build:guides`.

export const GUIDE_MARKDOWN: Record<string, string> = {
  "checks": "# What is a check?\n\nA **check** is StepCanary's watch on one of your automations. You create one check for each Zap,\nscenario, or workflow you care about, and that check's only job is to notice when the automation\nstops behaving normally and tell you.\n\n## What a check does\n\n1. **It gives you a private URL**, called the ping URL. Nobody else has it.\n2. **Your automation calls that URL every time it finishes.** You add one HTTP step at the end of\n   the automation to do this. Each call is a \"ping,\" and it means \"I just ran.\"\n3. **The check knows what normal looks like.** You tell it (\"this should run at least every two\n   hours\"), or it learns by watching for two weeks.\n4. **When normal stops, the check alerts you.** If the pings stop arriving, arrive far less often\n   than usual, or start carrying blank data, you get an email, Slack, or Discord message that says\n   which automation, what was expected, and what happened.\n\nThink of a check as a coworker who expects a quick \"done\" message from your automation on a\nregular basis, and who comes to find you when the message doesn't show up.\n\n## Why you need one\n\nAutomation tools are good at telling you when a step **fails with an error**. They are bad at\ntelling you when nothing happens at all, because silence isn't an error:\n\n- Someone turned the automation off, or it was paused after a billing problem.\n- A connected account expired, so the trigger quietly stopped firing.\n- A form was edited, and now every run sends an empty email field to your CRM.\n- A filter was changed, and only a tenth of the usual records get through.\n\nIn every one of these cases the tool shows no errors and sends no warning. You usually find out\ndays later, when a customer asks why nobody called them back. A check turns that silence into an\nalert within minutes.\n\n## What a check never does\n\n- **It never signs in to your tools.** There is nothing to connect or authorize. The automation\n  calls us; we never call it.\n- **It never stores your data.** By default a check records only when each ping arrived. If you\n  turn on payload monitoring, it also records field names and whether each field was filled in,\n  never the contents.\n- **It never changes, runs, or repairs your automation.** It only watches and reports.\n- **It never slows your automation down.** The extra step is one small web request that is\n  answered right away.\n\n## Quick reference\n\n| Term | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **Check** | StepCanary's watch on one automation. One check per Zap, scenario, or workflow. |\n| **Ping** | The signal your automation sends each time it finishes: \"I just ran.\" |\n| **Ping URL** | The private web address a check listens on. You paste it into the HTTP step. |\n| **Interval** | \"This should ping at least every X.\" The simplest way to describe normal. |\n| **Schedule** | \"This should ping at these set times,\" for automations that run on a timetable. |\n| **Volume** | \"This runs a lot; learn what's normal and tell me when it drops.\" |\n| **Grace period** | Extra time a check waits after a ping is due before it alerts you. |\n| **Active window** | The days and hours a check counts. Outside them its timer is paused, so quiet nights and weekends never alert. |\n| **Payload shape** | The names of the fields your automation sends, and whether each is filled in. Never the values. |\n| **Alert channel** | Where alerts go: email, Slack, Discord, or a webhook. |\n| **Monitoring gap** | A period when StepCanary itself couldn't vouch for its monitoring. We show this instead of sending an alert that might be false. |\n\n## What the statuses mean\n\n| Status | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **New** | The check is waiting for its first ping. It never alerts in this state. |\n| **Up** | Pings are arriving as expected. |\n| **Late** | A ping is overdue, but still inside the grace period. No alert yet. |\n| **Down** | The grace period ran out, or the automation reported a failure. You have been alerted. |\n| **Paused** | You (or a plan change) switched the check off. It keeps its history and sends no alerts. |\n\n## How many checks do I need?\n\nOne for each automation whose silence would cost you something: lead capture, order syncing,\ninvoicing, client reporting. A check that is paused doesn't count against your plan.\n\n## Set one up\n\nPick your tool: [Zapier](/guides/zapier), [Make](/guides/make), [n8n](/guides/n8n), or\n[Pipedream](/guides/pipedream). Each guide takes about three minutes.\n",
  "zapier": "# Monitor a Zap with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and a paid Zapier plan\n(Professional, Team, or Enterprise). The step uses **Webhooks by Zapier**, which Zapier doesn't\ninclude on its Free plan, and Free Zaps are limited to a trigger plus one action anyway.\n\n**New to StepCanary?** A check is our watch on one automation: it listens for a signal each time the\nautomation runs and alerts you when the signals stop. [What is a check?](/guides/checks)\n\nThe idea: add one last step to your Zap that tells StepCanary \"I ran.\" If those pings stop\narriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n**What it costs you in Zapier:** the extra step uses one task each time the Zap runs. Testing the\nstep in the editor is free.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the Zap, e.g. \"New lead → CRM\".\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the step to your Zap\n\n1. Open the Zap in the Zapier editor.\n2. Click the **+** below the **last** step.\n3. Search for and choose **Webhooks by Zapier**.\n4. For **Event**, choose **POST**, then **Continue**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Zapier editor, choosing Webhooks by Zapier with the POST event ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Payload Type** | `JSON` |\n| **Data** | Optional. See \"Watch the data too\" below. Leave empty for a simple heartbeat. |\n| **Headers**, **Basic Auth** | Leave empty |\n| Any other fields | Leave them as they are |\n\n`[ Screenshot: Webhooks by Zapier POST step with URL and Payload Type filled in ]`\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Test step** in Zapier.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received.\"** No refresh needed.\n3. **Publish** the Zap.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nThat is all. StepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived, so nothing can fire\nwhile you are still setting up.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your Zap… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Runs on a schedule (e.g. every morning at 9 AM) | **Schedule** mode with the same times, and 15-30 minutes of grace. Zapier's schedule trigger can start a few minutes late. |\n| Polls for new items (most Zapier triggers) and normally finds something every hour or so | **Interval** mode. Pick an interval comfortably longer than the longest normal quiet spell. |\n| Only gets activity in business hours (lead forms, bookings) | **Interval** mode with an **active window**, e.g. Mon–Fri, 9:00 AM–6:00 PM, so nights and weekends never alert. |\n| Runs dozens or hundreds of times a day | **Volume** mode: StepCanary learns what is normal for each weekday and tells you when it drops. |\n\nRemember that the ping only happens when the Zap **runs to the end**. A polling Zap that finds\nnothing new does not run, so does not ping. Choose the interval with that in mind.\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nZaps often keep running while a mapped field quietly goes blank. To catch that:\n\n1. In the **Data** section of the webhook step, add a row for each field you care about:\n   type a name on the left (e.g. `email`) and map the real field from an earlier step on the right.\n2. In StepCanary, edit the check and turn on **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape.\"**\n\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each one is filled in, blank, or a different\ntype. It never stores the values.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Data section of the webhook step with two mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures right away (optional)\n\nBy default you find out about a failure when the pings stop. Zapier's **custom error handling**\n(Professional plans and higher) lets you report one immediately:\n\n1. On the step most likely to fail, open the three-dot menu and choose **Add error handler**.\n2. On the error path, add a **Webhooks by Zapier** step with the **POST** event and the URL\n   `…/p/your-token/fail`.\n\nStepCanary then alerts right away instead of waiting for pings to stop. Things to know: an error\nhandler can't be added to the trigger or to a Paths step, turning one on switches off Zapier's\nautoreplay for that Zap, and Zapier stops sending its own error emails for errors the handler\ncatches.\n\n## No Webhooks by Zapier? Use a code step\n\nIf your plan includes **Code by Zapier** but you'd prefer not to use the webhook step, add a\n**Code by Zapier** step with the **Run Javascript** event as the last step, and paste this:\n\n```javascript\n// Optional: add rows under \"Input Data\" (for example, email) to send fields to StepCanary.\nconst url = \"https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/YOUR-TOKEN\";\ntry {\n  const res = await fetch(url, {\n    method: \"POST\",\n    headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n    body: JSON.stringify(inputData || {}),\n  });\n  return { pinged: res.ok, status: res.status };\n} catch (err) {\n  return { pinged: false, error: String(err) };\n}\n```\n\nThe step is written so that a problem reaching StepCanary never fails your Zap. It also uses one\ntask per run.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after Test step | The URL must be the whole ping URL with nothing added or missing. Copy it again with the **Copy** button. |\n| Zapier shows a 404 for the step | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| \"Malformed payload\" warning on the check | A mapped value contains a character that broke the JSON. The ping still counted. Setting Payload Type to `JSON` and using the Data rows (not a hand-typed body) avoids this. |\n| Alerts when the Zap is fine | The interval is shorter than a normal quiet spell. Lengthen it, add an active window, or switch to Volume mode. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The Zap is running more than 60 times a minute. Check for a Zap that triggers itself. |\n| Filters or Paths in the Zap | When a Filter stops a Zap, no later steps run, so no ping is sent. The same goes for steps on a Path that wasn't taken. Put the ping step where it will run for every case you count as \"working.\" |\n",
  "make": "# Monitor a Make scenario with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and a Make scenario.\n\n**New to StepCanary?** A check is our watch on one automation: it listens for a signal each time the\nautomation runs and alerts you when the signals stop. [What is a check?](/guides/checks)\n\nThe idea: add one last module to your scenario that tells StepCanary \"I ran.\" If those pings\nstop arriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n**What it costs you in Make:** the extra module uses one credit each time it runs.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the scenario.\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the module\n\n1. Open the scenario in the Make editor.\n2. Click the **+** after the **last** module.\n3. Search for **HTTP** and choose **Make a request**.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Make editor, adding the HTTP \"Make a request\" module at the end ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\nMake has two versions of its HTTP app, and the field names differ. The version is shown in the\nupper-right corner of the module's settings (**V4**, or **Version 3** for the older one).\n\n**HTTP (V4, the current version)**\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **Authentication type** | `No authentication` |\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Method** | `POST` |\n| **Headers**, **Query parameters** | Leave empty |\n| **Body content type** | `application/JSON` |\n| **Body input method** | `JSON string` |\n| **Body content** | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| **Parse response** | `No` |\n\n**HTTP (Version 3, legacy)**\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **URL** | Your ping URL |\n| **Method** | `POST` |\n| **Headers**, **Query String** | Leave empty |\n| **Body type** | `Raw` |\n| **Content type** | `JSON (application/json)` |\n| **Request content** | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n| **Parse response** | `No` |\n\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP \"Make a request\" module (V4) with the settings above ]`\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Run once**.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received.\"**\n3. Save the scenario and switch it to **Active** with the toggle at the top right.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nStepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your scenario… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Runs at set times | **Schedule** mode with the same times and 10-15 minutes of grace. |\n| Runs every N minutes and always completes | **Interval** mode, a little longer than the scenario's own interval. |\n| Is triggered instantly by a webhook, mostly in business hours | **Interval** mode with an **active window**. |\n| Processes many items a day | **Volume** mode. |\n\nIf the first module returns nothing (no bundles), Make ends the scenario there, so the ping\nmodule doesn't run. That means the ping only happens on runs that had something to do. Pick an\ninterval that allows for quiet runs.\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nTo be told when a mapped field goes blank or missing:\n\n1. In the HTTP module (V4), set **Body input method** to `Data structure` and add the fields you\n   care about, mapping each value from an earlier module. Make builds valid JSON for you, even\n   when a value contains quotes or line breaks.\n2. In StepCanary, edit the check and turn on **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape.\"**\n\nOn the legacy Version 3 module, set **Body type** to `Application/x-www-form-urlencoded` and add\nthe fields as key/value rows instead.\n\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each is filled in, blank, or a different type.\nIt never stores the values.\n\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP module body with mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures right away (optional)\n\n1. Right-click the module most likely to fail and choose **Add error handler**.\n2. On the error route, add another **HTTP → Make a request** module with the same settings, but\n   the URL `…/p/your-token/fail`.\n3. End the error route with the error handler you normally use.\n\nStepCanary then alerts as soon as the error route runs, instead of waiting for pings to stop.\n\n`[ Screenshot: error handler route with an HTTP module pointing at /fail ]`\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after Run once | Did the run reach the HTTP module? Check the bubble above it. Then check the URL is the whole ping URL. |\n| The HTTP module shows a 404 | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| \"Malformed payload\" on the check | A mapped value broke the JSON. The ping still counted. Use `Data structure` as the body input method (see \"Watch the data too\"). |\n| Alerts when the scenario is fine | The interval is too short for runs that have nothing to process. Lengthen it or add an active window. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The scenario is pinging more than 60 times a minute, usually because the HTTP module sits after an iterator and runs once per item. Move it after the aggregator, or to the end of the scenario. |\n",
  "n8n": "# Monitor an n8n workflow with StepCanary\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and an n8n workflow,\non n8n Cloud or self-hosted. A self-hosted instance must be able to make outbound HTTPS requests.\n\n**New to StepCanary?** A check is our watch on one automation: it listens for a signal each time the\nautomation runs and alerts you when the signals stop. [What is a check?](/guides/checks)\n\nThe idea: add one last node to your workflow that tells StepCanary \"I ran.\" If those pings stop\narriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n## Fastest way: import a ready-made template\n\nEach template is a small n8n workflow with the node already configured and a note explaining\nwhat to do. Download one, then in n8n open the Workflow menu and choose **Import from File**.\nCopy the StepCanary node into your own workflow and connect it after your last node.\n\n- [Heartbeat ping](/templates/n8n/stepcanary-heartbeat.json): the standard setup.\n- [Heartbeat ping with watched fields](/templates/n8n/stepcanary-heartbeat-with-fields.json): also sends fields, so StepCanary can tell you when one goes blank.\n- [Failure reporter](/templates/n8n/stepcanary-failure-reporter.json): a separate error workflow that reports a failure right away.\n\nIf you opened this guide from one of your checks, the downloads already contain that check's\nping URL. Otherwise, replace the placeholder URL in the node with your ping URL after importing.\n\nPrefer to set it up by hand? Follow the steps below.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the workflow.\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the node\n\n1. Open the workflow in the n8n editor.\n2. Click the **+** after the **last** node and add an **HTTP Request** node.\n\n`[ Screenshot: n8n canvas with an HTTP Request node added at the end ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\n**Parameters tab**\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **Method** | `POST` |\n| **URL** | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| **Authentication** | `None` |\n| **Send Query Parameters**, **Send Headers** | Off |\n| **Send Body** | On |\n| **Body Content Type** | `JSON` |\n| **Specify Body** | `Using JSON` |\n| **JSON** | `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` |\n\n**Settings tab**\n\n| Field | What to enter |\n|---|---|\n| **On Error** | `Continue` |\n| **Retry On Fail** | On, with **Max. Tries** set to `2` (optional) |\n\nSetting **On Error** to `Continue` means that if StepCanary were ever unreachable, your\nworkflow would still finish normally. Monitoring should never be the thing that breaks it.\n\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP Request node parameters ]`\n`[ Screenshot: HTTP Request node Settings tab with On Error = Continue ]`\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Execute step**.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received.\"**\n3. Save the workflow and **Publish** it. (On older n8n versions, switch it to **Active**.)\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nStepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your workflow… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Starts from a **Schedule Trigger** | **Schedule** mode with the same cron expression and timezone, and 5-10 minutes of grace. |\n| Starts from a **Webhook** or app trigger with steady traffic | **Interval** mode, longer than the longest normal quiet spell. |\n| Only gets traffic in business hours | **Interval** mode with an **active window**. |\n| Handles many executions a day | **Volume** mode. |\n\nA Schedule Trigger runs in the workflow's timezone, which you can see under Workflow menu →\n**Settings** → **Timezone**. Use that same timezone for the check.\n\nIf the workflow has an **IF** or **Switch** node, the ping only happens on branches that reach\nthe HTTP Request node. Connect every branch you count as \"working\" to it.\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nTo be told when a field goes blank or missing:\n\n1. Change **Specify Body** to `Using Fields Below` and add one row per field you care about,\n   with an expression as the value, e.g. name `email`, value `{{ $json.email }}`.\n2. In StepCanary, edit the check and turn on **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape.\"**\n\nUsing fields (not hand-written JSON) lets n8n escape quotes and line breaks for you.\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each is filled in, blank, or a different type.\nIt never stores the values.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Body parameters with mapped fields ]`\n\n## Report failures right away (optional)\n\nn8n can run a separate workflow whenever this one fails.\n\n1. Create a new workflow with an **Error Trigger** node followed by an **HTTP Request** node:\n   Method `POST`, URL `…/p/your-token/fail`.\n2. Save it. An error workflow doesn't need to be published.\n3. In the workflow you are monitoring, open the Workflow menu → **Settings** and choose that\n   workflow as the **Error Workflow**.\n\nStepCanary then alerts as soon as an execution fails, instead of waiting for pings to stop.\nUse one error workflow per monitored workflow, so each reports to its own check.\n\nn8n only runs error workflows for automatic executions. Running the workflow by hand in the\neditor won't trigger it, so don't be surprised if a manual test does nothing.\n\n`[ Screenshot: workflow settings with Error Workflow selected ]`\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after executing the node | Check the node's output panel for the response. `{\"ok\":true}` means it reached us; then confirm you are looking at the right check. |\n| The node returns 404 | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| Connection or timeout errors on self-hosted n8n | The server cannot reach the internet over HTTPS. Check its firewall or proxy settings. |\n| Alerts when the workflow is fine | The interval is shorter than a normal quiet spell, or the check's timezone differs from the workflow's. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The node runs once per item for a large batch. Open the node's **Settings** and turn on **Execute Once**, or put it after the loop. |\n",
  "pipedream": "# Monitor a Pipedream workflow with StepCanary\n\n**Important:** Pipedream has announced that Pipedream Workflows shuts down on March 31, 2027, and\nit no longer accepts new signups. This guide is for people who already have Pipedream workflows\nand want them watched until they move elsewhere.\n\n**Time:** about 3 minutes. **You need:** a StepCanary check (its ping URL) and an existing\nPipedream workflow.\n\n**New to StepCanary?** A check is our watch on one automation: it listens for a signal each time the\nautomation runs and alerts you when the signals stop. [What is a check?](/guides/checks)\n\nThe idea: add one last step to your workflow that tells StepCanary \"I ran.\" If those pings stop\narriving, StepCanary emails you.\n\n## 1. Create the check\n\n1. In StepCanary, click **New check** and name it after the workflow.\n2. Choose how often it should run (see \"Which settings should I pick?\" below) and create it.\n3. Leave the check page open. It shows your **ping URL** and \"Listening for your first ping…\".\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page with the ping URL and the \"Listening\" panel ]`\n\n## 2. Add the step\n\n1. Open the workflow in the Pipedream builder.\n2. Click the **+** below the **last** step.\n3. Add a **Node.js** code step.\n\nWe recommend a code step over Pipedream's no-code HTTP action. With the no-code action, a\nfailed request cancels the whole workflow, so a problem reaching StepCanary would mark your run\nas failed. The code below can't do that.\n\n`[ Screenshot: Pipedream builder, adding a Node.js code step at the end ]`\n\n## 3. Copy these settings\n\nReplace the step's code with this:\n\n```javascript\nimport axios from \"axios\";\n\nexport default defineComponent({\n  async run({ steps, $ }) {\n    try {\n      const resp = await axios({\n        method: \"POST\",\n        url: \"https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/YOUR-TOKEN\",\n        headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n        data: { status: \"ok\" },\n        timeout: 10000,\n        validateStatus: () => true, // never throw on an error status\n      });\n      $.export(\"stepcanary_status\", resp.status);\n    } catch (err) {\n      // Network problem or timeout: note it, and let the workflow finish normally.\n      $.export(\"stepcanary_error\", err.message);\n    }\n  },\n});\n```\n\n| Line | What to change |\n|---|---|\n| `url` | Your ping URL, e.g. `https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/…` |\n| `data` | Optional. See \"Watch the data too\" below. |\n\nIf you'd still prefer the no-code route, add **Send any HTTP Request** with method `POST`, your\nping URL, and a JSON body of `{\"status\":\"ok\"}`.\n\n## 4. Test it\n\n1. Click **Test** on the step.\n2. Look at the StepCanary check page. Within a couple of seconds it changes to\n   **\"Connected! First ping received.\"**\n3. **Deploy** the workflow.\n\n`[ Screenshot: StepCanary check page showing \"Connected! First ping received\" ]`\n\nStepCanary sends no alerts until the first ping has arrived.\n\n## Which settings should I pick?\n\n| Your workflow… | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Starts from a **Schedule** trigger | **Schedule** mode with the same cron expression and timezone, and 5-10 minutes of grace. |\n| Starts from an HTTP or app event trigger with steady traffic | **Interval** mode, longer than the longest normal quiet spell. |\n| Only gets traffic in business hours | **Interval** mode with an **active window**. |\n| Handles many events a day | **Volume** mode. |\n\nIf an earlier step ends the workflow early (for example with `return $.flow.exit()` or an\n**End Workflow** step), later steps do not run and no ping is sent for that event. Place the\nping step where it runs for every case you count as \"working.\"\n\n## Watch the data too (optional, Pro)\n\nTo be told when a field goes blank or missing, send the fields you care about. In the code\nabove, change the `data` line:\n\n```javascript\n        data: {\n          email: steps.trigger.event.body.email,\n          order_id: steps.trigger.event.body.id,\n        },\n```\n\nThen edit the check in StepCanary and turn on **\"Alert me when the data it sends changes shape.\"**\nStepCanary only looks at field names and whether each is filled in, blank, or a different type.\nIt never stores the values.\n\n## Report failures right away (optional)\n\nIn a Node.js step, wrap the risky part of the workflow and report the failure before rethrowing:\n\n```javascript\nimport axios from \"axios\";\n\nexport default defineComponent({\n  async run({ steps, $ }) {\n    try {\n      // ... your logic ...\n    } catch (err) {\n      await axios.post(\"https://ping.stepcanary.com/p/YOUR-TOKEN/fail\").catch(() => {});\n      throw err;\n    }\n  },\n});\n```\n\nStepCanary then alerts right away instead of waiting for pings to stop.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Problem | What to check |\n|---|---|\n| The check stays on \"Listening…\" after Test | Look at the step's exports. `stepcanary_status` of `200` means it reached us; then confirm you are looking at the right check. |\n| `stepcanary_status` is `404` | The URL is not a valid ping URL. Copy it again from the check page. |\n| Alerts when the workflow is fine | The interval is shorter than a normal quiet spell, or the check's timezone differs from the trigger's. |\n| \"Runaway loop detected\" | The workflow is running more than 60 times a minute. Check for a workflow that triggers itself, or a source emitting far more events than expected. |\n",
};

